#elif DEFER_SSS
// ---- SKIN SUBSURFACE SCATTERING (17; RenderVKSkinSSS; LLCleanRendererVK::recordSkinSss) ----
// Screen-space subsurface scattering of the Bakes-on-Mesh skin pixels (the G-buffer variant
// GB_VAR_SKIN, llvovolume.cpp cleanBomSkinBake), after the lighting (combine, or BASE + LOCAL),
// before the forward passes. Only the DIFFUSE light scatters: the lit kernels stored each skin
// pixel's diffuse irradiance in uSssDiff (sssExport -- the light divided by the diffuse colour, as
// the final image carries it: haze / fade slope included), so
//   out = lit + diffuse colour x (scattered irradiance - irradiance)
// keeps the specular, the emissive and the haze's in-scatter where they were and puts the
// albedo back at the pixel itself (post-scatter texturing: the bake's detail stays sharp).
// What scatters: ambient (probes + GI), sun / moon, the stochastic lights' and emitters' diffuse
// share; the deterministic local lights only on legacy faces with neither specular nor env (the
// walk is not split into its shares).
// PROFILE: the six-Gaussian fit to measured skin of d'Eon & Luebke (GPU Gems 3, ch. 14, 2007):
// variances in mm^2, per-channel weights summing to 1 -- red reaches ~8 mm, blue stays at the
// point (x RenderVKSkinSSSScale: the push's scale).
// SEPARABLE PER LANE (the separable screen-space formulation of Jimenez et al. 2009 / 2015, split
// by lobe; our own implementation): two passes, HORIZONTAL (this kernel without CLEAN_SSS_VERTICAL:
// uSssDiff -> uSssTmp) then VERTICAL (CLEAN_SSS_VERTICAL: uSssTmp -> the lit output). Every 2D
// Gaussian is exactly the product of its 1D marginals and is radially symmetric, but the product
// a(x) a(y) of a SUM of Gaussians is not: its cross terms (a narrow lobe across, a wide one along)
// make a plus-shaped kernel, so a diagonal shadow edge got up to ~30 % less of the far red tail
// than an axis-aligned one and a lit corner 0.2 - 0.6 x the radial profile's (red, 2 - 6 profile
// mm). So each channel's lobes are split into LANES of similar width, each convolved separably on
// its own and summed with its weight in the vertical pass:
//   red   A = lobes 1-4, B = lobe 5, C = lobe 6     (corner / diagonal within ~10 % of radial)
//   green A = lobes 1-2, B = lobes 3-6
//   blue  A = lobes 1-6 (one lane: the profile sits under a pixel or two; within 0.01 absolute)
// Six lanes in two RGB9E5 words per pixel: SSS_CDF1 / SSS_LANE_W1 = (A.r, A.g, A.b), SSS_CDF2 /
// SSS_LANE_W2 = (B.r, C.r, B.g) (uSssTmp: two R32UI texels per pixel). Each lane's 1D kernel is
// its lobes' normalised sum; its one-sided CDF (profile mm, per lane; C(inf) = 1/2) is tabulated
// at x_j = SSS_RADIUS_MM (j / 64)^3 (dense at the centre, the last node holds the tail: 1/2) and
// read with linear interpolation in that cube-root coordinate (max error 0.0017, the C lane;
// staged in shared memory). Generated offline; scripts/check/checks/skin_sss_separable.py
// rebuilds them from SSS_VAR / SSS_W / SSS_LANE_LOBES and fails on a mismatch.
// TRUE REACH (no whole-kernel shrink): profile mm -> px from this pixel's step along the surface
// (the shorter skin neighbour step in the pass's direction, a step over SSS_TILT_MAX x the screen
// step counting as an edge; at least the screen step) x 1000 / scale, and the kernel keeps that
// width at any distance. A workgroup stages its run plus an apron as wide as its widest pixel's
// reach (atomicMax, uniform), one texel per entry up to SSS_ENT_MAX entries, then 2 / 4 / 8
// texels per entry (box sums: still exact integrals) -- only past SSS_BLOCK_MAX-texel entries
// (~3400 px) does a pixel's kernel narrow to fit.
// BINS FROM PREFIX SUMS: the staged run holds fixed-point prefix sums (2^29 over the run's
// brightest entry x its length: exact integer differences, no cancellation in the dark) of the
// lane values and the skin count. A bin [r0, r1] px weighs, per lane, the profile's mass over it
// (C(r1) - C(r0)) times the mean difference from the centre over it -- (sum - centre x skin count)
// / width, an exact integral (linear inside a texel), so a hard shadow edge moving through any bin,
// however wide, gives a continuous, monotone response (no staircase). Bins per side: every texel
// within SSS_DENSE_PX px on its own, then SSS_TAIL bins of quadratically growing width to the
// reach (the last to infinity). Measured offline against the exact convolution of a 1000:1 step:
// max error 0.3 % of the lit level up to 700 px reach, 0.6 % at 1500 px (4-texel entries).
// The centre's own bin [-0.5, 0.5] px is implicit (every sum is a difference from the centre):
// every pixel's lane weights sum to 1.
// DEPTH BARRIERS (replace the old follow-surface weights, which pulled taps back by their distance
// from the centre's tangent plane and so also cut the spread on curved skin: a cheek's sagitta over
// the 32 mm reach at scale 4 is ~half the 16 mm tolerance): an entry boundary is a barrier where
// the eye distance jumps by more than SSS_TILT_MAX x the lateral step between neighbouring skin
// entries (looking back across up to SSS_GAP non-skin entries, so a hair strand, a lash line or a
// glasses frame over the skin is no barrier unless the skin on its two sides is at different
// depths; a non-skin gap wider than that -- an eye, a sleeve, the background -- is a barrier),
// or inside an entry of several texels. A pixel integrates only up to the nearest barrier on each
// side (binary search over the barrier prefix count): the nose's silhouette over a cheek, a hand in
// front of the face. Curvature never trips it. A non-skin texel (hair, clothes, background, off
// screen) holds no value and no skin count, so it contributes the centre: nothing outside the skin
// bleeds in.
// EXACT NO-OP on constant irradiance: the fixed-point sums are differences from the centre's own
// quantised value (zero), the horizontal pass packs at the centre's own shared exponent (its word
// comes back unchanged) and the vertical pass sums sum_lane w (lane - irradiance), never
// sum_lane w lane - irradiance. Deterministic: fixed bins, no rotation, no random numbers, no
// history.
// Push: scale x 1000 (bits0-15), diagnostic mode (16-18): 0 normal, 1 mask, 2 abs delta x16,
// 3 the irradiance S0, 4 REACH (the kernel's own metric, both passes: hue = the reach in px by
// octave, SSS_DBG_BAND; brightness = screen step / surface step of the steeper axis, full on
// face-on skin, dim toward the SSS_TILT_MAX tilt limit; white = a barrier boundary at this pixel
// (either pass); dark hatching = a barrier inside the reach truncates the kernel; black lines =
// the world 10 mm grid), 5 RULER (the normal result with the world mm grid over the skin: thin
// dark lines every 1 mm where they are >= 4 px apart, green every 10 mm; magenta = a vertical
// pass barrier boundary -- the horizontal pass's words carry the real lanes here). Reach px = 8 profile mm = 8 x scale world mm, so px / mm = reach / (8 x scale). The
// grid is the world's axis planes, drawn per family only where the skin's normal is within ~53 deg
// of perpendicular to that axis, so a cell is within 25 % of its size (never smaller): the red
// fringe's width in mm can be counted off the skin in mode 5, and mode 4 shows whether the kernel
// agrees (the kernel itself: scripts/check/test_skin_sss_replica.py).
#define SSS_DENSE_PX 16                       // texels per side that are each their own bin
#define SSS_TAIL 16                           // bins per side over the reach beyond SSS_DENSE_PX
#define SSS_CDF_N 64                          // lane CDF table intervals
#define SSS_RADIUS_MM 8.0
#define SSS_TILT_MAX 4.0                      // a neighbour step > 4 x the screen step is an edge, not a slope
#define SSS_GAP 64                            // non-skin entries a barrier test looks back across
#define SSS_ENT_MAX 896                       // staged entries per run (the run + both aprons)
#define SSS_K_MAX 4                           // entries per thread in the scan: ceil(SSS_ENT_MAX / SSS_SEG)
#define SSS_BLOCK_MAX 8                       // texels per entry at the widest reach
